course: dictionary
lesson: 9138
title: Snapshots in context
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A snapshot is a quick picture of something at one moment. It is taken fast, and it does not show what came before or after. The context shows you which kind of snapshot the author means.
explain: «A poll is a snapshot of today: people change their minds, some do not answer, and some who say they will vote never do; turnout means how many actually vote.» The clue is «of today». A poll shows what people think now, not what they will think next week.
explain: Snapshot can mean a photo, a quick look, or a saved copy of a computer. In each case it freezes one moment. Ask yourself: does this show one moment, or the whole story? If it is one moment, the word snapshot fits.
fact: A snapshot is a photo taken quickly and casually, not posed carefully.
fact: A snapshot can also be a quick look at what something is like at one moment, such as a snapshot of life in Victorian times.
fact: In computing, a snapshot is a saved copy of how a system or files looked at one moment, so you can go back if something goes wrong.
fact: A survey on one day is a snapshot, so its results can change on other days.
exercise: «A poll is a snapshot of today.» What does snapshot mean here? | A long film that shows the whole story | A photo that has been posed carefully for hours | A list of everyone who will vote next year | *A picture of how things are at one moment
exercise: Which of these is a snapshot in computing? | A film of files being made over a whole year | A new game you download to play | *A saved copy of how files looked at one moment | A list of every file ever deleted
exercise: Lily says her holiday photo is a snapshot. Why? | It was posed carefully for a long time | *It was taken quickly and shows one moment | It shows the whole holiday from start to end | It is a painting
test: «A poll is a snapshot of today: people change their minds...» Why might the results change tomorrow? | Because polls never use numbers | Because the poll was printed in colour | Because a snapshot is always a painting | *Because people can change their minds
test: In the poll sentence, what does turnout mean? | *How many people actually vote | How many people answer the phone | How much people like the poll | How long the poll lasts
exam: Why is a survey on one day only a snapshot, not the full picture? | It is a photo of a holiday | It is always correct for everyone | It covers every day of the year | *It shows one moment, so results can change on other days
